ext("C019", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): REQ_NEGATIVE_HABIT 2 (5.00★) — negative / inverse boards, both German, 17 months apart, same design (smoking; unfulfilled days as the streak) — 'opens the quit-habit use case the product currently cannot serve'.")
ext("C006", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): PRAISE_SIMPLICITY 60 (34.88%, mean 4.98★ — the highest of any theme over 20) described by what is NOT there ('no frills, no 20 taps to log something'; 'No bloat. Just one page for all essentials'; 'no extra gamification, no ads, no BS'; 'Die App erfüllt genau ihren Zweck und nicht mehr'); U:anti_bloat 66 (38.37%, 4.98★); §8.5 do not add gamification, streak pressure, social feeds or AI — 'the product's position IS restraint'; do not rewrite the UI (68.02% craft praise).")
ext("C178", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): 'Others are way too expensive or overly animated and cute. This one strikes a great balance'; PRAISE_NO_GAMIFICATION 2 ('Minimal incentive to follow some arbitrary set of rules to make habits stick, leaving it up to the user instead'); a GitHub-contribution-grid identity (7 mentions at 5.00★, developers self-identify: 'As a SWE'; 'I'm a developer so I can definitely understand the GitHub feel'); U:competitive_win 27 (15.70%, 4.93★): 'Tried them all and this is by far the best'.")
ext("C024", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): the counter-position — no imposed gamification is praised (PRAISE_NO_GAMIFICATION 2, 5.00★) and zero reviews ask for social, leaderboard or AI features; v2.4.0 added a toggle to disable streak/consistency stats.")
ext("C185", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): PRAISE_DESIGN 71 (41.28%, 4.87★) and PRAISE_NATIVE_FEEL 11 ('looks and feels like an app made by Apple'; 'even Apple apps don't feel this good'; 'Love the adoption of Liquid Glass') — craft praise persisted at 58.5% even in the era where the mean fell to 4.02★: 'the decline is additive — the same praise, now with something attached to it'.")
ext("C264", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): 'no frills, no 20 taps to log something' — logging friction as the stated reason for choosing it; UX_BULK_ENTRY 1 ('If I want to add 10 check-ins to a previous day it's very painful!', a 5★ whose whole body is the complaint); DEF_CHECKIN_SPAM 1 asks that a second tap undo a check-in.")
ext("C223", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): REQ_UNDO_CHECKIN / DEF_CHECKIN_SPAM — 'Check in too many times' (4★): a second tap should undo; v2.0.1 restored a long-press check-in configurator.")
ext("C010", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): back-dated entry exists but bulk back-entry is painful (UX_BULK_ENTRY 1, 5★).")
ext("C064", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): price level is not the barrier — MON_PRICE_FAIR 10 (5.81%, 5.00★) including four written after the price rose from $10 to €23 ('der Preis für die Vollversion sehr fair'; 'Simple, barata y eficaz'), against U:price_objection 5 (2.91%, 3.20★: '18€ is way too much for a tracker lmao, pc games cost less than this', 1★; 'etwas teuer', 5★); the five objectors are Italian, Slovak, German, German and Czech — high-income European markets; 'A price cut would buy less than four sentences of disclosure'; the load-bearing case: a German 4★ who 'wanted to buy to support the developer' and did not at €23 'for a single app with one function'.")
ext("C004", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): a $10 one-time price was named as the reason to choose the app over subscription rivals; at €23 the same product loses a supporter-motivated buyer ('23 Euro sind doch recht viel für eine einzelne App mit einer Funktion') while ten others still call it fair — the objection is the multiple, not the level.")
ext("C062", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): high-spend (fixed list, 82 reviews) 4.62★ vs rest of world 4.58★ — noise; money-negative slightly higher in the high-volume group (India, Spain) on 6–8 reviews; the five price objectors are all European; 'this corpus does not support' the claim that price sensitivity concentrates in low-income markets; the one-star mass sits in the US (6 of 11) and the single-review long tail has zero 1★ at 4.50★ — the opposite of the usual small-corpus pattern.")
ext("C231", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker, 172 reviews): 937 public ratings vs 172 text (18.4%), 4.781★ vs 4.599★; France 57 ratings / 2 text; the distribution is prompted (in-app review request) and the tail is inverted — 11 one-star, 6 at 2★+3★ — 'the signature of a transaction failing rather than a product gradually underperforming'; 10 edited reviews carry their last-edit date; 5 rating-vs-text contradictions (a 5★ 'given in advance', a 5★ whose body is only a complaint, a 1★ that calls it a 'Great app'); a 'six seven' meme review kept; the rating decline (4.89 → 4.02) cannot be separated from a composition shift as the YouTube/Reddit launch cohort aged out.")
ext("C002", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): mean 4.89 → 4.19 → 4.02 across three eras while craft praise held at 58–76% and COMP_BEST is awarded in the final review — the fall is entirely money (widget gated, subscription added, price ×2.3), broken purchases, a review prompt and a post-overhaul regression: 'Only 2 of 11 one-star reviews are about what the app charges; 6 of 11 are a promise not kept'.")
ext("C058", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): seven reviews name YouTube or Reddit as the acquisition channel ('The dev took feedback from me on Reddit and released my requested feature within a couple of weeks'); PRAISE_INDIE 9 (5.00★) with no entry after 2025-02-28 and CH_YOUTUBE none after 2025-11-25 — 'the early corpus is full of people who followed a developer; the recent corpus is full of people who found an app'; §8.4.4 rebuild the acquisition story or accept a harsher audience.")
ext("C239", " Report 79 (Ripples / Checker): U:developer_equity 36 (20.93%, 4.94★) — a solo, named, Reddit/YouTube-facing developer with a public roadmap was a purchase motive (MON_SUPPORT_DEV 3: 'because I like your approach'; 'to support the developer'); the equity eroded 26.4% → 12.2% as the audience changed, and the one paying customer who reports being ignored writes 'Developer doesn't respond. Waste of time' in a 1★.")
